eant to be  backboards objects devices that were  never meant to move people  to see people just go to work and to  bring patients to us at  to me those are the heroes  I was assigned to be there that night  I didn't pay to go there  these people paid to go there these fans  paid to be there they paid for an  experience and  um they had like many they had the  opportunity to to evacuate and get out  of there get out of Harm's Way and they  didn't  and some didn't until you more or less  had to force them to leave  that's that's heroism  um  I think there was a bond that's that's  you know that that's been created really  amongst our community when it comes to  the EMS community  um I think there's you know moments  where  you know the people that were there that  night and the people that responded that  night  um you know there's a moment where the  first ambulance showed up  and it had been what felt like an  eternity for ambulances to show up in in  our world when resources aren't showing  up it's because the scene's not safe  and so hearing those sirens in the  distance thinking to yourself these  resources are going to be held back  and then all of a sudden that resource  shows up that ambulance shows up and  there is no hesitation from that crew  that they look you in the eye as you  make eye contact and you immediately  know that  we're working we're going to work we got  to get this done and to hear words from  fellow colleagues saying put as many as  you can back here  is that something that would happen on a  day-to-day basis  yeah I think so  um you know typically when you call  9-1-1 and ambulance responds there may  be one patient and maybe two patients  any more than that more ambulances come  and take those one or two patients but  to take as many critical patients that  this particular crew took that night  that for me was a  that was a defining moment of that night  um  I think that night for me overall and  and the days that followed  as a as a kid I always had a passion for  medicine I always wanted to to do  something medicine related I had dreams  of being a doctor of you know whatever  that looked like  um but I always thought it'd be cool to  be in an ambulance how cool would it be  to drive up and down the strip with  lights and sirens on like how cool would  that be and so straight out of high  school was given that opportunity  and over the years you know my  what I saw myself doing in medicine  shifted  um whether that was to to go to medical  school those thoughts shifted those  thoughts changed I second guessed it I  you know reconsidered it but one thing  that never changed and one thing that  for me was solidified that night  was that  I have been placed where I'm at to help  people  and that night solidified that whatever  I do in my life I want to help people  and whether that's through medicine  whether that's through you name it  I want to be there for people when it's  their moment when they're at what they  believe is the lowest of low in their  their time of need I want to be that  person and for me it's given me a drive  to explore how how do we how do we  better serve our community how do I  better serve my community  that that moment solidified that  and I'm constantly reminded of that  every time I see a sticker that says  Vegas strong  and you know unfortunately I think  things happen in our society that  various hashtags are created various  phrases are created  and growing up here in southern Nevada  there's  a lot of people don't realize that  there's Community outside of  the glitz the glamor the entertainment  the amazing stuff that our city does but  there is such a community here  it's a community that I've been  fortunate to grow up in it's a community  that I've attended College in it's a  community that I've been given the  opportunity to be able to give back in  that Vegas strong just for me as cliche  as it sounds has such a significant  meaning  for me it's an even more significant  meaning that I have an opportunity to  work at a company called community  to be able to serve my community  I I agree with you the biggest strong  hits different  it's tattooed on the back of my leg it  just  it feels different than the ones that  come and go after  yep whatever else in the days and weeks  that followed how did you guys  um take care of yourselves  yeah you know uh  believe it or not you'll meet a lot of  type a person Personalities in this  business  people that don't want help  people that are more worried about  everyone else besides themselves  and one of the things that makes me  proud to be able to work for this  organization was that from the moment  that this occurred to that morning  following the hours long that we were on  that scene  we brought in counselors we brought in a  therapists  and I can tell you that you know it's uh  EMS doesn't have it figured out First  Responders don't have it figured out  but I can tell you that this company did  everything it could  to  ensure that mental health and the  well-being of all of our employees was  taken care of  I think it's something we still struggle  with today  um it's a  you know unfortunately we still continue  to lose people to to mental health  issues in our industry  and We Know It Takes a toll on on our  Personnel  um  you know for me personally it it took a  while it took a while to crack the crack  the show for me to open up and to be  able to share  and to be able to figure out why I was  having the thoughts I was having or or  having the visions that I was having  um  but yeah there's a  it's a focus of ours it's it's something  that we deeply care about here in our  organization and you know we we  constantly try to find ways and whether  they be subtle whether they be more  direct to really focus in on the mental  health and the mental well-being of not  only our leaders but of our  men and women that every day don't know  what they're going to respond to don't  know what they're going to experience  and  truth be told don't know if they're  gonna  make it home that night  in  five years  what has changed for the better  um  you know I think there's a you know five  years later I think there's  there's a handful of things that have  changed I think from a from an industry  standpoint  there's a little bit  I think there's more emphasis to have  the uncomfortable conversations  being the entertainment capital of the  world we attract a lot we attract big  events and  When We Gather in a room with ourselves  with stakeholders and Community Partners  um  you're now in a room where we can have  those difficult conversations nobody  wants to talk about what happens if  there's a bomb what happens if there's  this device what happens if somebody  decides to do something bad and some of  them are I think easier to talk about as  opposed to others  um I think there's this underlying  I don't think I know you know in our  world in that first responder world  there's lots of conversation about  what's going to happen next  not if it's going to happen but when is  it going to happen and so I think  there's a you know there's a more  emphasis from from the industry from  from what we're doing to ensure that the  safety of all the of all those that live  here visit here and and go to these  events because  at the end of the day  Evil's not going to prevail  five years later evil has not prevailed  there's been some challenging things  that have happened in our communities  some bad things that have happened in  our community but I think five years  later  we're still  we're still this tight-knit community  we're still here to look out for one  another  we're still we're still Vegas strong  personally what's changed  um you know up until up until a week ago  it was it's  still trying to wrap my my head around  that it's been five years  it's you know it's  it's  difficult  it's you know lots of reflection you  know that night unfortunately I lost a  friend who's amongst the one of the 58  that that passed  um and and those thoughts come back  this year was the first year that  I went back through text messages that I  had from five years ago everything from  people that were checking in on me to  the text that I sent to to my family  to the text that I sent to to somebody  that died  and that was the first time in in the  five years I'm sure I looked at him you  know post post the event but probably  the first time that kind of stirred  these emotions that  you know  in those  I think we have a tendency to  compartmentalize where we put feelings  and where our emotions are stored and  for me five years later it it kind of  it's opened up  some of those  but for me it again it reminds me of the  community that I'm a part of it reminds  me of the opportunity I have to be able  to do what I do to be able to sit down  and be able to share  my story to be able to share the story  of the hundreds and thousands of people  in our community that helped that night  for those that are complete strangers to  our community that every year they come  back  and want to  they're a part of this unique Community  that's that's been created after such a  horrific event  anything  I mean we can obviously talk about  the role the public played in helping  you their ingenuity  their willingness to stand there beside  you  I mean how cool would it be if  it's a community to come together again  make sure you know your CPR trained to  make sure your kids know how to swim  make sure you're donating blood we could  talk about  all of those things that just as being a  good Community person are important  but I think I can kind of write to some  of that sure I just want to make sure  that you're feeling okay about it and  that you're saying everything  that you want to say if there's anything  that stood out or  yeah no you know I think you know when  it comes to  our communities stood up for the  the minutes and the hours and the days  and the weeks and the years since that  event there was a  there was a it was a it was a different  and it still is  um there was almost a turn  you know for for somebody you know  walking into a Starbucks in uniform  somebody holding the door open for you  and just saying thank you  um you know it's  it's uh it's easy for me to thank people  um but when people were thinking me it  was  maybe to this day it's hard you know and  I I don't I don't stand behind the  um what's what you're paid to do it's  it's what you signed up for  nobody that wears our uniform signed up  for that night nobody that wears our  uniform signs up to get shot at nobody  signs up for this career to to deal with  demons and have nightmares is it a part  of the job it's become part of the job  but  um yeah the community just really showed  up that night and you know it's I think  it's just important that  we have these difficult conversations  you know stop the bleed training where  we teach people how to put tourniquets  on and  how you can  truly save somebody's life those are  tools that came out of bad experiences  you know we learn a lot of this stuff  because of war and that's a tool that  you know we use in EMS and it was a tool  that that night was an important tool  and you know a very Vivid thought that  night was seeing folks that would take  blankets that would take stethoscopes  that were taking seat belts and turning  them into tourniquets because it's such  an important tool and uh yeah I mean I  you know our  I truly believe that we're all placed  where we are for a reason we have bad  things that happen in life we have a few  bad sour apples in our in our society  but we're a community that is here to  look out for one another and support one  another and anything we can do to to  make ourselves better in turn makes our  community that much stronger  I don't believe so  uh
 

